## Python

::: {#aa95f15f .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy.linalg import hilbert

ns = range(2, 15)
conds, errors = [], []
for n in ns:
    H = hilbert(n)
    xhat = np.linalg.solve(H, H @ np.ones(n))
    conds.append(np.linalg.cond(H))
    errors.append(np.max(np.abs(xhat - 1)))

plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.5))
plt.semilogy(ns, conds, "o-", label="condition number")
plt.semilogy(ns, errors, "s-", label="error in solution")
plt.semilogy(ns, np.array(conds) * 1.1e-16, "--", label="condition number × ε/2")
plt.xlabel("n")
plt.legend()
plt.show()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![](solutions-5_files/figure-html/cell-2-output-1.png){width=512 height=319 fig-alt='Log-scale plot against n from 2 to 14. The condition number of the Hilbert matrix rises in a straight line from about 10 to 10 to the 18; the error in the solution rises in parallel from about 10 to the minus 16, closely following the dashed line of condition number times epsilon over 2.'}
:::
:::


## MATLAB

